<article class="env-lemma" id="07KX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07KX">Lemma <span data-tag="07KX">15.88.9</span>.</a> Let $K = (K_ n^\bullet )$ be an object of $D(\textit{Ab}(\mathbf{N}))$. There exists a canonical distinguished triangle </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K \to \prod \nolimits _ n K_ n^\bullet \to \prod \nolimits _ n K_ n^\bullet \to R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K[1]  \]
</div>
  <p> in $D(\textit{Ab})$. In other words, $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K$ is a derived limit of the inverse system $(K_ n^\bullet )$ of $D(\textit{Ab})$, see Derived Categories, Definition <a href="/tag/08TC" data-tag="08TC">13.34.1</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose that for each $p$ the inverse system $(K_ n^ p)$ is right acyclic for $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $. By Lemma <a href="/tag/07KW" data-tag="07KW">15.88.1</a> this gives a short exact sequence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n K^ p_ n \to \prod \nolimits _ n K^ p_ n \to \prod \nolimits _ n K^ p_ n \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       for each $p$. Since the complex consisting of $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n K^ p_ n$ computes $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/07KW" data-tag="07KW">15.88.1</a> we see that the lemma holds in this case. 
    <p>
      Next, assume $K = (K_ n^\bullet )$ is general. By Lemma <a href="/tag/07KW" data-tag="07KW">15.88.1</a> there is a quasi-isomorphism $K \to L$ in $D(\textit{Ab}(\mathbf{N}))$ such that $(L_ n^ p)$ is acyclic for each $p$. Then $\prod K_ n^\bullet $ is quasi-isomorphic to $\prod L_ n^\bullet $ as products are exact in $\textit{Ab}$, whence the result for $L$ (proved above) implies the result for $K$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>